# SaaS override, no-domain variant: plain HTTP bound to the VM's public IP,
# not HTTPS. Mounted in place of ./Caddyfile.saas by docker-compose.saas-ip.yml,
# layered on top of docker-compose.saas.yml — backend/postgres stay unpublished
# (ports: [] from docker-compose.saas.yml) exactly like the with-domain SaaS
# case, without relying on a host firewall as the only thing standing between
# them and the internet (see docs/DECISIONS.md #54 for why the earlier plan —
# plain docker-compose.yml plus a manually-run ufw rule — was rejected).
#
# If you have a real domain instead of a bare IP, use Caddyfile.saas — it
# gets automatic Let's Encrypt HTTPS with no changes to that file needed.
# This file exists only for the no-domain case, where that isn't available.
#
# Why HTTP and not `tls internal` (self-signed, like the appliance Caddyfile
# uses for localhost/127.0.0.1): `tls internal` was tried here first and
# failed in a real deploy (Vultr VM, bare public IP, no domain). Caddy's own
# logs confirmed it minted a self-signed cert successfully
# ("certificate obtained successfully, issuer: local"), but every connection
# still failed the TLS handshake — curl: "error:0A000438:SSL routines::tlsv1
# alert internal error"; Chrome: ERR_SSL_PROTOCOL_ERROR — and Caddy logged
# "server is listening only on the HTTPS port but has no TLS connection
# policies; adding one to enable TLS" on every single attempt, meaning it
# could never actually select the policy/cert it had just minted. Restarting
# the container, force-recreating it, and connecting with an explicit
# https:// scheme did not change the outcome, and the Caddyfile mounted
# inside the container was confirmed to match this file.
#
# Root cause: TLS SNI (RFC 6066) only carries a hostname. There is no
# hostname to send when a client connects to a bare IP literal, so curl and
# Chrome both send no SNI extension at all in that case. Caddy picks which
# site's TLS policy/certificate to serve for an incoming connection by
# matching SNI against the configured site address — with no SNI in the
# ClientHello, it has nothing to match {$SITE_ADDRESS} against, so it can't
# serve the certificate it already has. This is a structural mismatch
# between how TLS clients behave against bare IPs and how Caddy selects
# certificates, not a config mistake in this file — it doesn't show up for
# the appliance Caddyfile (localhost/127.0.0.1, where local tools do send
# hostname SNI) or for Caddyfile.saas (a real domain always has a hostname
# to send as SNI). See docs/DECISIONS.md #55 for the full rationale and the
# downsides accepted by shipping HTTP here instead.
#
# SITE_ADDRESS comes from the environment (docker-compose.saas-ip.yml passes
# it from .env) — the VM's public IP, e.g. 203.0.113.10. Routing logic below
# is otherwise identical to Caddyfile / Caddyfile.saas.
http://{$SITE_ADDRESS} {
    handle_path /api/* {
        reverse_proxy backend:8000
    }

    handle {
        reverse_proxy frontend:8080
    }
}
